From 61d408020d9c4fc0df08ad13fba51c4abae65cde Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 03:36:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ntsync: add GameNative's userspace ntsync to proton-cachyos
 11 bionic

Ports the userspace ntsync backend (GameNative/ntsync-android) onto
The412Banner's Android port of Proton-CachyOS 11 (1233149d, their
build-proton-cachyos-11.0-20260703 test build).

The eight ntsync patches from the GE-Proton 11.0-5 ntsync build apply to
this tree with a plain git apply, and seven produce the same code as
there: this tree's patched server/inproc_sync.c, esync and ntdll files
are identical to the GE-11.0-5 ones. They go into android/patches/ntsync/
and are applied last, after every Android patch.

server/inproc_sync.c additionally fixes the esync/ntsync mix-up behind the
"turn esync off or the container hangs at a black desktop" note on the
11.0-5 ntsync build. With WINEESYNC=1 (Winlator's default) wineserver
enabled esync at startup and told clients the backend was esync, but
get_inproc_device_fd() still picked ntsync and the object code ran esync
branches for it. Now:

- the ntsync probe is split out of get_inproc_device_fd() and exported as
  ntsync_available();
- wineserver's do_esync() is false when ntsync is usable, so ntsync wins
  and PROTON_NO_NTSYNC=1 brings esync back;
- the client turns esync on only when wineserver reports
  ESYNC_USED_BY_SERVER, instead of reading WINEESYNC on its own.

build-step-arm64ec.sh gains --build-ntsync-android and the new patches,
plus MARKERS rows so a patch that goes missing fails the build.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019oF8nnfMD8z5Z1MeNXoYS9
